# 10. SUNLIGHT, SKY, AND VOLUMETRIC GOD RAYS

Implement a coherent sun-and-sky lighting system.

The sun must be a defined directional light source with a visible solar disc in the sky. Its apparent position must agree with the direction of sunlight illuminating the world.

The sun must cast appropriate shadows and change position according to the game's day/night cycle.

Implement volumetric light scattering so sunlight creates visible shafts when partially occluded by trees, branches, mountain ridges, clouds, and structures.

The intended effect should resemble the cinematic golden-hour sunlight in the reference image.

Requirements:

- Sun position follows the existing time-of-day system.
- Sunlight direction, sky appearance, and shadows agree.
- Volumetric shafts emerge naturally around occluding geometry.
- Forests create irregular shafts between trees and branches.
- Mountains interact naturally with the atmosphere.
- Fog and atmospheric scattering vary with distance and weather.
- Rays fade naturally instead of appearing as obvious rectangular screen-space artefacts.
- The scene does not contain permanent artificial beams unrelated to occlusion.

Use a suitable volumetric implementation, such as ray marching, froxel-based volumetrics, or a carefully implemented screen-space approximation, depending on the renderer.

Do not falsely describe screen-space light shafts as physically accurate volumetric ray tracing.

The sun must function as a genuine scene light source rather than a bright sphere pasted into the sky.

Implement coherent lighting for dawn, midday, dusk, night, and changing weather.

# 11. RAY TRACING: VERIFY REAL SUPPORT BEFORE IMPLEMENTING

I want ray-traced visual quality wherever the existing engine and graphics stack can support it.

First inspect the actual graphics API, engine version, browser capabilities if applicable, and available GPU features.

If the game uses standard Three.js WebGL, do not pretend that adding a checkbox or naming a shader enables hardware-accelerated ray tracing. Standard WebGL does not expose a general-purpose hardware ray-tracing pipeline.

If the project uses WebGPU, verify the actual availability of relevant ray-tracing extensions or supported techniques. Do not assume hardware ray tracing is automatically available merely because the renderer uses WebGPU.

If the game runs in a native engine with a supported hardware ray-tracing API, investigate genuine ray-traced reflections, shadows, or global illumination.

Where real hardware ray tracing is unavailable, implement the best practical hybrid rendering solution:

- High-quality shadow mapping.
- Screen-space reflections.
- Ray-marched or screen-space ambient occlusion.
- Probe-based environment reflections.
- Light probes or baked indirect illumination where practical.
- Volumetric scattering.
- Contact shadows.
- PBR materials.
- Realistic sky illumination.
- Appropriate temporal accumulation where stable.

Provide graphics settings such as Standard, High, and Ultra where appropriate. Any ray-tracing option must accurately report whether genuine hardware ray tracing is available or a fallback is being used.

Do not invent APIs, claim unsupported features work, or sacrifice the entire renderer to an experimental implementation.

# 12. REALISTIC GRASS AND NATURAL GROUND VEGETATION

Replace simplistic random green blades with a coherent ecosystem of grass, shrubs, flowers, ferns, reeds, fallen leaves, and other vegetation appropriate to the biome.

Grass must not consist of identical upright green triangles scattered uniformly over the landscape.

Implement a vegetation system with:

- Multiple grass species and blade shapes.
- Variation in height, width, curvature, and orientation.
- Tapered blade silhouettes.
- Natural clusters and irregular density.
- Species-dependent colour distributions.
- Appropriate material response and translucency where supported.
- Small patches of bare soil, gravel, stones, and leaf litter.
- Density influenced by terrain slope, elevation, moisture, soil, and biome.
- Reduced grass on exposed rock, steep cliffs, railway ballast, and disturbed ground.
- Natural transitions among grass, shrubs, soil, and forest floor.

Use instanced geometry or an appropriate GPU-driven vegetation technique.

Near the camera, vegetation should have convincing silhouettes and depth. At medium distances, use lower-detail instances. At long distances, use stable density representations and terrain-material blending rather than attempting to render every individual blade.

Avoid obvious circular density boundaries, repeated clumps, uniform spacing, and visible LOD transitions.

Grass must not obstruct the railway, cover functional controls, or create accidental player collision.

# 13. WIND-DRIVEN VEGETATION AND A LIVING ENVIRONMENT

Implement a unified environmental wind system driven by world-space wind direction, wind strength, gusts, and biome conditions.

Wind should affect:

- Individual grass blades.
- Tall grass and reeds.
- Shrubs.
- Flexible branches.
- Tree crowns.
- Leaves and lightweight debris.
- Hanging vegetation.
- Other appropriate lightweight environmental objects.

Vegetation must not simply rotate as rigid objects. Use vertex-shader deformation or equivalent techniques that bend flexible geometry according to height, stiffness, and wind direction.

Grass should bend near its base and move more strongly toward the tips. Trees should exhibit subtle, slower crown movement. Strong gusts should create larger coordinated movements.

Wind direction and animation must remain spatially coherent across neighbouring terrain chunks. Avoid visible motion discontinuities at chunk boundaries.

Wind intensity should respond to biome and weather. Heavy trunks and rigid structures must remain appropriately stable.

Use world-coordinate-based animation so vegetation does not visibly reset when chunks stream or LOD levels change.

Wind must not arbitrarily push the train unless the existing physics system explicitly supports that gameplay mechanic.

Make the environment feel alive through appropriate environmental audio and subtle interactions, not excessive animation of every object.

